│ patch_name               │ seeded_local_search_heilbronn11                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the placeholder (all points at origin, giving score 0) with a deterministic       │
│                          │ optimization: initialize 11 points in the equilateral triangle (vertices plus an interior │
│                          │ spread), then run a seeded hill-climbing local search that repeatedly perturbs the        │
│                          │ vertices of the currently-worst triangle and accepts only improvements to the global      │
│                          │ minimum triangle area. Points are clipped back into the triangle (y >= 0, y <= √3·x, y <= │
│                          │ √3·(1−x)) after every perturbation. The minimum area over all C(11,3)=165 triples is      │
│                          │ computed vectorized with NumPy, and the RNG is seeded so the output is fully              │
│                          │ reproducible. This directly maximizes the primary metric (min_area_normalized) while      │
│                          │ remaining fast (~0.5–1 s) and robust: if anything fails, it returns the last feasible     │
│                          │ (clipped) configuration, and a pure NumPy fallback grid is used as the initialization.    │
