                        Patch Metadata - Gen 10/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ softmin_gradient_maximin_optimizer                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the placeholder (all-zero) point set with a deterministic gradient-based max–min  │
│                          │ optimizer for the Heilbronn 11-point problem.                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key ideas:                                                                                │
│                          │ 1. **Initialization**: the three triangle vertices plus 8 seeded pseudo-random interior   │
│                          │ points (vertices are known to help push the minimum area up; seed makes it                │
│                          │ deterministic).                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Smooth-min (softmin) objective**: the min-triangle-area objective is non-smooth, so  │
│                          │ we minimize f = tau·log(Σ exp(-a_m/tau)) over all C(11,3)=165 triplet areas, annealing    │
│                          │ tau from 0.02 down to ~0.0004. As tau→0 this converges to the true minimum area.          │
│                          │ 3. **Exact analytic gradient**: for each triplet the signed area is a bilinear cross      │
│                          │ product, so ∂f/∂p is computed exactly and vectorized with `np.add.at` — no finite         │
│                          │ differences, keeping eval time well under a second over ~1200 Adam steps.                 │
│                          │ 4. **Feasibility projection**: after each Adam step, points are converted to barycentric  │
│                          │ coordinates, clipped to the simplex, and mapped back — guaranteeing every iterate stays   │
│                          │ inside the equilateral triangle.                                                          │
│                          │ 5. **Error handling**: the whole optimization is wrapped in try/except; on any failure it │
│                          │ falls back to a simple, valid deterministic configuration, so the function always returns │
│                          │ a feasible (11,2) array.                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This turns the metric from 0.0 into a genuine optimized configuration targeting           │
│                          │ combined_score near or above 1.0.                                                         │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
