│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ vectorize_point_scores_and_strengthen_refine                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current hill-climber wastes most of its time in a triple-nested Python loop inside    │
│                          │ `point_scores` (called every iteration over all 165 triples × 3 points), and only tries   │
│                          │ one candidate move per scale for the whole group of worst points. Two targeted fixes:     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. Vectorize `point_scores` using `np.minimum.at` scatter-min, eliminating the Python     │
│                          │ triple loop. This frees eval_time budget for more iterations.                             │
│                          │ 2. Strengthen the search: try multiple independent random moves for each of the worst     │
│                          │ points individually at each scale, and add a finer final scale level plus more            │
│                          │ iterations. Moving one point at a time with several attempts is much more effective for   │
│                          │ maximin problems than perturbing 3 points simultaneously, since the objective is limited  │
│                          │ by the single binding smallest triangle.                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Both changes keep determinism (fixed seeds) and error handling intact.                    │
