╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ fast_barycentric_incremental_sa                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ The current version is both slow (10.6s: full O(n^3) area recomputation plus a            │
│                          │ Python-loop projection every step) and weak (0.38). Key improvements:                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Barycentric coordinates**: work in (u,v) simplex coords (u≥0, v≥0, u+v≤1).           │
│                          │ Projection is a two-line vectorized clamp, and the affine map to the equilateral triangle │
│                          │ is exact — no slow per-point edge/vertex closest-point search.                            │
│                          │ 2. **Incremental scoring**: precompute the 165 triplet index list once. When one point    │
│                          │ moves, only the C(10,2)=45 triplets containing that point change; update just those areas │
│                          │ with a small vectorized op instead of all 165 each of 24k iterations.                     │
│                          │ 3. **Anisotropic randomized directions**: per the recommendation, use random directional  │
│                          │ moves (random unit direction, multiple step scales per sweep) plus random jitter,         │
│                          │ regenerated each iteration — this squeezes through asymmetric gaps better than a fixed    │
│                          │ fan.                                                                                      │
│                          │ 4. **Multi-restart with greedy polish**: try several deterministic seeds (vertices +      │
│                          │ spread interior layouts), anneal each briefly, then greedily polish the best with         │
│                          │ decreasing scales. Keep a static feasible fallback for error safety.                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This should raise min_area_normalized well above 0.02 while cutting runtime to ~1–2s.     │
