│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_bottleneck_polish                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The current Adam-softmin ascent treats all near-minimal triangles with smoothly annealed  │
│                          │ weights, but it terminates at whatever local optimum the single random seed reaches. The  │
│                          │ key insight from the Heilbronn problem is that the optimum is dictated by a handful of    │
│                          │ *binding* (near-degenerate) triangles: progress stalls because the softmin gradient mixes │
│                          │ many weak constraints. I add a post-Adam polishing phase that (1) computes exact minimum  │
│                          │ areas, (2) identifies a *set* of "tight" triangles whose area is within an adaptive       │
│                          │ threshold (3.0× min early → 1.2× min late) of the bottleneck, and (3) moves the vertices  │
│                          │ of those triangles along the exact analytic gradient of their signed areas with a         │
│                          │ temperature-scaled step and line-search acceptance. Early iterations move near-binding    │
│                          │ points broadly (exploration), late iterations focus only on the exact binding constraints │
│                          │ (fine polish). This mirrors the proven temperature-scaled move magnitudes and costs       │
│                          │ almost nothing. I also add a small deterministic multi-start (3 seeds) and keep the best  │
│                          │ result, raising the chance of landing in the best basin while keeping runtime well under  │
│                          │ a second.                                                                                 │
