├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ bottleneck_coordinated_moves                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current anneal/polish only moves single uniformly-random points. The key structural   │
│                          │ weakness: the minimum-area triplet ("bottleneck") typically requires *correlated*         │
│                          │ movement of 2+ of its member points (e.g., sliding two points apart along the triangle    │
│                          │ side) — independent single-point perturbations can't escape such traps. I add a new       │
│                          │ refinement stage that: (1) computes each point's bottleneck membership count (how many    │
│                          │ near-minimal triplets contain it), (2) picks points proportionally to that count rather   │
│                          │ than uniformly, and (3) occasionally moves the top-2 bottleneck points simultaneously     │
│                          │ along the *same* correlated direction (plus a correlated "spread along the bottleneck     │
│                          │ edge" move). This is cheap because incremental area updates already exist; I reuse the    │
│                          │ `contains` structure by recomputing areas for the union of affected triplets when 2       │
│                          │ points move. This directly implements the recommended idea while keeping determinism      │
│                          │ (fixed seeds) and error handling unchanged.                                               │
