│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ bottleneck_gradient_reheat                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ All programs plateau around min_area 0.02 because single-point pattern search gets stuck  │
│                          │ in shallow local optima: moving one point at a time is myopic when the minimum-area       │
│                          │ (bottleneck) triangle constrains 3 points simultaneously. The known strong heuristic for  │
│                          │ Heilbronn-type problems is to move the points of the *smallest* triangles along their     │
│                          │ signed-area gradients (all three vertices of a bottleneck triangle together), which       │
│                          │ directly raises the constrained minimum. I add: (1) a vectorized bottleneck gradient move │
│                          │ (move up to 3 bottleneck points along the gradient of their triangle areas), (2) an       │
│                          │ annealing reheat loop per seed that restarts from the best-so-far configuration when      │
│                          │ progress stalls (targeting the 0.02 plateau), and (3) keep the pattern-search polish as a │
│                          │ final refinement. This directly addresses the plateau at 0.02 and should push             │
│                          │ combined_score above 1.0 while keeping determinism and modest runtime.                    │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
