│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ bottleneck_polish_and_targeted_moves                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The primary problem is quality, not speed: min_area_normalized is stuck at 0.01 while the │
│                          │ benchmark is 0.0365. Random jitter local search easily gets trapped: the minimum-area     │
│                          │ ("bottleneck") triangle involves specific points, and random single-point moves rarely    │
│                          │ relieve it. Expert practice for Heilbronn-type max-min problems is (a) to identify the    │
│                          │ bottleneck triangle each iteration and move only its vertices with a multi-scale          │
│                          │ line/grid search (this is far more effective than blind jitter), and (b) to finish with a │
│                          │ fine polish pass on the bottleneck. I add a `_bottleneck` helper, a `_polish` routine     │
│                          │ that performs coordinate-wise multi-scale moves on the bottleneck triangle's points       │
│                          │ (accepting only strict improvements), and hook it after the local search for each seed.   │
│                          │ This uses only ~12 candidate evaluations per bottleneck step, keeping runtime comparable  │
│                          │ while dramatically improving the achievable minimum area. I also add one extra structured │
│                          │ (near-symmetric) seed, since symmetric configurations are known to be good starting       │
│                          │ points for Heilbronn problems.                                                            │
