│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ softmax_projected_ascent_multistart                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of SLSQP on a log-sum-exp softmin with hard simplex constraints (which frequently │
│                          │ fails/stalls and produced score 0.00 here), this approach uses a fundamentally different  │
│                          │ optimizer: projected gradient ascent directly on a softmax-weighted area objective with a │
│                          │ barrier-free parameterization. Each iteration computes all 165 triangle areas, forms      │
│                          │ softmax weights at low temperature (heavily emphasizing the bottleneck triangles), and    │
│                          │ takes a gradient step that explicitly pushes bottleneck points apart along the direction  │
│                          │ that increases their areas. Feasibility is maintained by barycentric clipping (u,v ≥ 0,   │
│                          │ u+v ≤ 1) after every step, a cheap exact projection onto the simplex per point. Multiple  │
│                          │ deterministic restarts, an annealed step size, and a final local "pair perturbation"      │
│                          │ refinement give robustness: if optimization fails, a graceful deterministic fallback grid │
│                          │ is returned. The bottleneck-focused gradient is far more effective at escaping the        │
│                          │ plateau than generic constrained SQP, and the whole pipeline is pure NumPy, fast and      │
│                          │ deterministic.                                                                            │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
