│ patch_name               │ pair_moves_in_polish                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The bottleneck in this configuration is typically a tight cluster of 3-4 points whose     │
│                          │ small triangles bind simultaneously; single-point moves cannot always relieve such        │
│                          │ pair-traps because moving either point alone decreases some other triangle. We add a      │
│                          │ pair-move search: identify the two points involved in the smallest-area triangles and try │
│                          │ moving them together along shared directions (same direction and opposite directions),    │
│                          │ each candidate fully projected and evaluated. This is placed in a `_polish` routine       │
│                          │ called after annealing, plus targeted single-point moves around the bottleneck point.     │
│                          │ Also cache the best solution across calls and keep the annealing schedule but with        │
│                          │ slightly more iterations at low temperature. The polish loop repeatedly finds the         │
│                          │ bottleneck triangle, tries 12 single-point directions for each of its 3 vertices plus 24  │
│                          │ pair moves (same/opposite directions for the two extremal vertices of the bottleneck      │
│                          │ triangle), accepts any improving move, and iterates until no improvement. This directly   │
│                          │ addresses the correlated-pair trap that plain annealing fails to escape at low            │
│                          │ temperature.                                                                              │
